eonard & Co., 1892), John C. Grant, editor — the semi-centennial volume of the congregation, read from the Princeton Theological Seminary copy digitised by the Internet Archive as item secondpresbyterian00chic","source_id":"second_presbyterian_chicago_1892","tier":2,"tier_label":"near-primary recollection","url":"https://archive.org/details/secondpresbyterian00chic","what_it_does_not_supply":["An 1835 residency, or any part of one. The earliest line on the roll is seven years after the scene date and the ratified ladder never makes an 1835 resident from a later appearance alone.","A woman's own name, much of the time. The roll sets a wife as `Mrs.` followed by her HUSBAND'S name — `Fullerton, Mrs. A. N.` — so a line that meets a man in the 1835 layer has met his name and not him, and the crosswalk flags every such line.","A death or a departure that can be dated. `Deceased` and `Dismissed` are the state of the roll at its closing on 1 June 1892, not events with dates of their own.","An address, a trade or a household. The roll carries none of the three."],"what_it_supplies":["2,255 lines of a Chicago church roll running from 1 June 1842 to 1892 — a name, how the member was admitted (profession or letter), the date, and for the closed roll a remark (dismissed, deceased, ordained).","962 distinct surnames, of which 938 lines carry a surname this project already holds from the town of 1835.","The founding cohort of 1 June 1842, which is where the overlap with the 1835 town is thickest: W. H. Brown, George W. Dole and Mrs A. N. Fullerton are all on the first day's roll.","A dated Chicago appearance for a surname, which is exactly the kind of second source the resident sweep files as a lead."]},"uses":["scene"]}
